From this visualization, it appears all variables are positively correlated with each other. In other words, as a person consumes more vitamin D, fiber, sugar, saturated fat, or cholesterol, they on average consume more of the other dietary components as well.

Let us examine the relationship between vitamin D consumption and depression, as evidenced by the variable "DPQ020." Scientists and physicians believe that vitamin D deficiency can contribute to depression, either causing it or worsening symptoms. 

```{r}
modified3_full_df <- modified2_full_df %>%
