The linearity assumptions are not met in this data set (Shapiro-Wilk normality test has p-value less than 0.05), so we will not fit a linear curve to show on the graph as well. What the graph does tell us, however, is that there is not an important relationship between vitamin D consumption and total fiber consumption, among both depressed individuals and people who do not meet criteria for clinical depression, at least according to these sample data. Fiber and vitamin D are both positive additions to a diet (meaning more, to a certain extent, is generally better), yet they are not related very strongly, as this plot suggests. 

