Accuracy of the k-nearest neighbors model is 0.194 (95\% CI: 0.1531, 0.2405), which is worse than guessing. This indicates that the model classifying almost everyone as not depressed when some individuals in fact have depression. An enormous number of people (off the main diagonal in the confusion matrix) fall into the category with 270 individuals, who do experience depression despite the model misclassifying these individuals as unaffected. The perfect specificity (100\%) and the fact that in the testing data, 271 people have depression versus 64 unaffected individuals, means that probably there are additionally errors in the splitting of training and testing data, as the training and testing sets should each have approximately equal numbers of depressed and not depressed survey participants, since that is the function of the "splitstackshape" package. In the actual survey data, only a minority of people experienced clinical depression, in contrast to the majority in this testing data set, indicating a problem. 

