Based on our findings, there is insufficient evidence to conclude that various aspects of diet (total sugar, total saturated fat, total cholesterol, daily intake of vitamin D, and total fiber content) can predict a depression diagnosis. A major limitation of the models run here is that they vastly over predict people as not having depression when in fact they do. If medical professionals wanted to draw conclusions between dietary status and depression diagnosis status, they would need to perform further research, perhaps incorporating more variables that are more closely linked to depression (such as anxiety or major life events, which are not data included in NHANES).

